' Component notes
note right of RateLimiter
  <b>Strategy Pattern Interface</b>

  Abstraction for rate limiting algorithms.

  <b>Implementations:</b>
  • TokenBucketRateLimiter: Allows bursts
  • SlidingWindowRateLimiter: Accurate tracking
  • (Extensible for other algorithms)

  <b>Key Methods:</b>
  • tryAcquire(key): Attempt to proceed
  • getAvailablePermits(key): Check quota
  • reset(key): Clear limits (admin/testing)

  <b>Design Principle:</b>
  Dependency Inversion - depend on abstraction
end note

note bottom of TokenBucketRateLimiter
  <b>Token Bucket Algorithm</b>

  Classic rate limiting approach used by
  AWS, Google Cloud, and other major platforms.

  <b>How It Works:</b>
  1. Bucket holds tokens (up to capacity)
  2. Tokens refill at constant rate
  3. Each request consumes 1 token
  4. If no tokens, request denied

  <b>Advantages:</b>
  • Allows burst traffic (up to capacity)
  • Simple and efficient O(1) operations
  • Smooth long-term rate limiting

  <b>Trade-offs:</b>
  • Less accurate than sliding window
  • Possible burst at refill time

  <b>Thread Safety:</b>
  ConcurrentHashMap + per-bucket locks
end note

note bottom of SlidingWindowRateLimiter
  <b>Sliding Window Algorithm</b>

  More accurate than fixed window,
  prevents boundary exploitation.

  <b>How It Works:</b>
  1. Store timestamp of each request
  2. On new request, remove expired timestamps
  3. Count remaining requests in window
  4. Allow if under limit

  <b>Advantages:</b>
  • No boundary reset issues
  • Accurate request counting
  • Prevents burst at boundaries

  <b>Trade-offs:</b>
  • Higher memory (stores all timestamps)
  • O(n) cleanup per request

  <b>Thread Safety:</b>
  ConcurrentHashMap + per-window locks
end note

note left of RateLimitConfig
  <b>Value Object - Configuration</b>

  Immutable configuration for rate limiting.

  <b>Builder Pattern:</b>
  Fluent API for flexible configuration

  <b>Predefined Configs:</b>
  • defaultConfig(): 100 req/min
  • strictConfig(): 10 req/min
  • permissiveConfig(): 1000 req/min

  <b>Parameters:</b>
  • requestsPerWindow: Max requests
  • windowDuration: Time window
  • blockOnExceeded: Wait or reject
  • maxWaitTime: Max blocking time

  <b>Immutability:</b>
  Thread-safe, shareable across threads
end note

note right of RateLimitFilter
  <b>Intercepting Filter Pattern</b>

  Servlet filter applying rate limiting
  to all HTTP requests.

  <b>Key Extraction Strategies:</b>
  • IP-based: By client IP address
  • User-based: By authenticated user ID
  • API Key-based: By API key header

  <b>HTTP Headers Set:</b>
  • X-RateLimit-Limit: Max allowed
  • X-RateLimit-Remaining: Remaining quota
  • X-RateLimit-Reset: Seconds until reset
  • Retry-After: When to retry (if exceeded)

  <b>Response Code:</b>
  429 Too Many Requests (RFC 6585)

  <b>Integration:</b>
  Works with any RateLimiter implementation
  (Strategy Pattern)
end note

note bottom of RateLimitExceededException
  <b>Domain Exception</b>

  Thrown when rate limit is exceeded.

  <b>Contains:</b>
  • Key that exceeded limit
  • Limit that was exceeded
  • Duration until retry allowed

  <b>HTTP Mapping:</b>
  Maps to 429 Too Many Requests

  <b>Retry-After Header:</b>
  Provides standard HTTP retry guidance
end note

note left of TokenBucket
  <b>Per-Key State</b>

  Internal state for each rate-limited key.

  <b>Algorithm Details:</b>
  • Refills continuously based on time
  • Uses double for fractional tokens
  • Lock prevents concurrent modification

  <b>Memory Efficient:</b>
  Only stores: capacity, rate, tokens, timestamp

  <b>Refill Strategy:</b>
  Lazy refill on each tryConsume() call
end note

note left of RequestWindow
  <b>Per-Key State</b>

  Internal state for sliding window tracking.

  <b>Algorithm Details:</b>
  • Queue stores request timestamps
  • Cleanup removes expired entries
  • Lock prevents concurrent modification

  <b>Memory Usage:</b>
  O(limit) per key - stores all timestamps

  <b>Cleanup Strategy:</b>
  Lazy cleanup on each tryAddRequest() call
end note

' Legend
note right of RequestWindow
  <b>Rate Limiting Architecture:</b>

  <b>Design Patterns Used:</b>
  • <b>Strategy Pattern:</b> RateLimiter interface with multiple algorithms
  • <b>Builder Pattern:</b> RateLimitConfig.Builder for flexible construction
  • <b>Intercepting Filter:</b> RateLimitFilter for cross-cutting concern
  • <b>Value Object:</b> Immutable RateLimitConfig
  • <b>Exception Handling:</b> RateLimitExceededException for domain errors

  <b>Rate Limiting Algorithms:</b>
  • <b>Token Bucket:</b> Allows bursts, constant refill rate
  • <b>Sliding Window:</b> Accurate tracking, no boundary issues

  <b>Key Extraction Strategies:</b>
  • <b>IP-based:</b> Rate limit by client IP (default)
  • <b>User-based:</b> Rate limit by authenticated user
  • <b>API Key-based:</b> Rate limit by API key

  <b>HTTP Standards Compliance:</b>
  • 429 Too Many Requests (RFC 6585)
  • X-RateLimit-* headers (de facto standard)
  • Retry-After header (RFC 7231)

  <b>Thread Safety:</b>
  • ConcurrentHashMap for key-to-state mapping
  • ReentrantLock per key for state modifications
  • No global locks (high concurrency support)

  <b>Performance Characteristics:</b>
  • Token Bucket: O(1) acquire, O(1) memory per key
  • Sliding Window: O(n) acquire, O(limit) memory per key

  <b>Use Cases:</b>
  • API rate limiting (prevent abuse)
  • DDoS protection (mitigate attacks)
  • Fair resource allocation (prevent monopolization)
  • Cost control (limit expensive operations)
end note

